If you have already booked, or are about to book, Malaga Event Gear's [Wedding Pack](/packages/wedding/), the question that actually matters now is not which rentals to choose, it is how to get the most out of the one you have. This guide is not a comparison or a sales pitch. It covers what to tell your technician in advance so the day runs the way you planned it. For the full picture of what we cover for weddings, see our [wedding rentals](/blog/wedding-rentals/) overview.

## Brief Overview

---

The single biggest lever you control after booking is information. The Wedding Pack includes a live technician who stays on site through the ceremony, the speeches and the dancing, adjusting sound and lighting as the day moves. What that technician cannot do is guess your run of show. Tell us the timing, the speaker order and any venue curfew in advance, and the technician plans around it instead of reacting to it mid event.

## Key Highlights

---

- The Wedding Pack's live technician stays on site through the whole event, not just for setup, so briefing them in advance is what actually makes that inclusion pay off.
- Reception speeches usually rotate through several speakers, best man, maid of honor, parents, sometimes the couple. That hand off is a technician's job, and it goes smoother with advance warning.
- A short checklist, grounded in what we actually ask for at booking, covers everything worth sending before your event.
- For the officiant's ceremony microphone choice, see our [unique wedding ceremony rentals](/blog/unique-wedding-ceremony-rentals/) guide. For curfews, wind and power outdoors, see our [outdoor wedding rental considerations](/blog/outdoor-wedding-rental-considerations/) guide. Neither is repeated here.

## Brief the Technician on Your Run of Show

The Wedding Pack's live technician is included specifically to handle technical control and engineering support throughout your event, not just to drop off equipment and leave. That value only shows up if the technician knows what your day actually looks like before it starts. Share your ceremony start time, when the reception begins, the order of speeches, the first dance moment, and any hard end time the venue sets. A technician working from that timeline can have levels set and cues ready before each moment arrives, rather than adjusting after a moment has already started.

This matters most at the transitions. Ceremony sound is quiet and intimate, tuned for a single voice at conversational volume. Reception sound moves through speeches at similar clarity, then up to dance volume later in the night. A technician who knows when each shift happens can move with it. One who finds out mid event is always half a step behind. We will not turn your run of show into a script for us to follow to the second, but the broad shape of the day is exactly what lets the technician do the job the Wedding Pack pays for.

<InlineCTA />

## Microphone Hand Offs for Multiple Reception Speakers

A wedding reception rarely has one speaker. Best man, maid of honor, parents, sometimes the couple themselves, all take a turn, often in an order that gets decided the week of the wedding rather than months ahead. The Wedding Pack includes professional wireless microphones for speeches and announcements, plural, and managing the hand off between speakers is where the live technician earns their keep.

Each speaker has a different voice, a different volume, a different distance they hold the mic from their mouth. A technician who is actually watching adjusts gain as the mic changes hands, catches the moment right before feedback rather than after it starts, and keeps a beat of silence from turning into dead air while one speaker sits down and the next stands up. None of that needs your input in the moment. What does need your input beforehand is the order, so the technician is not guessing who speaks next.

If your reception has five or more speakers, or a mix of toasts and a longer speech from the couple, ask about adding a second wireless microphone when you book. Every package can be extended with add ons, including extra microphones, and having a second one ready means one speaker is not waiting for another to physically hand theirs over.

For the officiant's own microphone during the ceremony itself, lapel versus handheld and why that choice matters more there than anywhere else in the day, see our [unique wedding ceremony rentals](/blog/unique-wedding-ceremony-rentals/) guide. That is a separate decision from the reception hand offs covered here.

## Ceremony to Reception: A Timing Note

The shift from ceremony sound to reception sound is one more thing worth flagging in advance rather than leaving to be figured out on the day, especially outdoors where wind, an open layout, or a venue curfew change what the technician can do and when. We cover that fuller outdoor picture, power, curfews and equipment placement, in our [outdoor wedding rental considerations](/blog/outdoor-wedding-rental-considerations/) guide. Here, the short version is: tell us roughly when the ceremony ends and the reception sound needs to be ready, and the technician builds the transition into the plan instead of scrambling for it.

## What to Send Us Before Your Event

This is the actual checklist, built from what we ask for anyway, not a generic wedding planning list.

- **Date, venue and guest count.** We already collect this to put a quote together, and it decides whether the [Eco Pack](/packages/eco/) or the Wedding Pack fits your event.
- **Your run of show.** Ceremony start time, reception start, speech order, first dance, and any hard curfew the venue sets.
- **Reception speaker order.** Who speaks, roughly when, and whether any of them will need a second microphone ready alongside the first.
- **Your officiant's microphone preference**, lapel or handheld, covered in full in our [ceremony rentals](/blog/unique-wedding-ceremony-rentals/) guide.
- **Any customization.** Extra microphones, extra technician hours or other add ons, so we can build the right configuration for your event rather than the default one.
- **Booking timing.** Services need at least 24 hours notice, so send this information as early as you can, ideally well before that minimum.

